Honour --disable-dsound. QEMU 9.2's guard is

    if not get_option('dsound').auto() or (host_os == 'windows' and have_system)

and `not auto()` is true for **disabled** as much as for enabled, so a
disabled option walks straight into the block and `dsound` is declared
anyway as soon as dsound.h is present. The option is then never consulted
again -- unlike its neighbour `coreaudio`, which passes
`required: get_option('coreaudio')` to `dependency()` and does the right
thing. So on a Windows (cross-)build --disable-dsound was silently
ignored: CONFIG_AUDIO_DSOUND stayed set, dsoundaudio.c was compiled into
libqemu-embed-i386.dll and -lole32 -ldxguid were linked, for a backend
nothing can reach (2ksbox's audio is patch 20's `embed` audiodev).

Skip the block outright when the option is disabled; `enabled` still
forces the check and its error, `auto` still detects on Windows.

diff --git a/meson.build b/meson.build
index 2a5da02834..e6f03be1f6 100644
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -1750,7 +1750,8 @@ if get_option('oss').allowed() and have_system
   endif
 endif
 dsound = not_found
-if not get_option('dsound').auto() or (host_os == 'windows' and have_system)
+if not get_option('dsound').disabled() and \
+   (not get_option('dsound').auto() or (host_os == 'windows' and have_system))
   if cc.has_header('dsound.h')
     dsound = declare_dependency(link_args: ['-lole32', '-ldxguid'])
   endif
